## 12. Per-request doctor identity and the flag authority (PR5)

1. **CC1-PERF.** `DoctorIdentityResolver::resolveForUser()` memoises its answer in the current ROUTED HTTP request's
   attributes, keyed by account id; outside a routed request (queue job, console command, direct service call)
   nothing is memoised. `DoctorAccountLinkService` calls `forget()` after every link, relink and unlink. Measured on
   PostgreSQL 16 (3 → 9 rows): the visit list went from 13 → 37 identity lookups (115 → 211 queries) to 1 → 1
   (104 → 174), the patient queue from 7 → 19 (64 → 112) to 1 → 1 (58 → 94). Every per-row authorization answer is
   unchanged. Never cache identity across requests or in a long-lived process.
2. **CC1-CG08.** `AndroidReleaseGovernanceScanner` reads the enforcement flag through
   `FeatureFlagService` (CG-08), never `config('feature_flags…')`. `enforcement_off` passes only when the registry
   entry DECLARES `default => false` (`declaredDefault()`; a missing default never reads as off, FA-06) AND the
   runtime value is off (an environment override counts, FA-07); an undeclared flag is still a FAIL. Its
   summary reports `governance_phase` as the phase it applies (unrecognised → `phase_4a`) and the declared value as
   `governance_phase_declared`.
3. **CC1-ODO is not changed.** An empty saved odontogram prints the designed empty state ("Belum ada kondisi
   odontogram yang dipilih.", Sprint 63.1 `StructuredOdontogramPrintTemplateTest` #6), not a blank chart. Refusing
   empty-chart prints would reverse that tested decision and needs an owner decision first.
4. Tests: `DoctorPatientScopeQueryBudgetTest` (token `DoctorPatientScope`), `DoctorDeviceAndroidFlagAuthorityTest`
   (token `DoctorDevice`), both declared in `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.

## 9. A signed prescription is changed only by its own doctor (owner decision 2026-11-16, amends rule 228 §12.2)

1. `PrescriptionAuthoringAuthority::signedContentLocked()`: once `doctor_signature_canvas_path` is set, only the
   account `maySign()` accepts for the prescription's doctor (its own `doctor_id`, else the visit's — the doctor the
   edit path derives) may change the prescription. Identity, not role: Super Admin, Owner and Supervisor RME that are
   not that doctor are refused; `Gate::before` lets Super Admin past the policy, so the service decides too.
2. Three layers. `RmePrescriptionPolicy::update` checks the scope (`view()`) FIRST, then the lock, so an out-of-scope
   account is refused for scope and learns nothing about the signature. `RmePrescriptionService::update()` re-reads the
   row after taking the visit lock (every prescription writer takes it first) and refuses with an
   `AuthorizationException` before anything is decoded or written. The page offers no edit to anyone else and says why.
3. A refusal for this reason writes one `RME_PRESCRIPTION_SIGNED_EDIT_REJECTED` row (ids, actor, reason code; no
   value, no patient data): from the controller when the policy's denial message is the lock, from the service after
   the rolled-back transaction otherwise. Other refusals write nothing (rule 228 §9: one row per write, unchanged).
4. The reverse race: the edit form carries `content_version` (`RmePrescriptionService::contentVersion()`, a fingerprint
   of the R/ canvas key and the audited fields). A signature submitted with a version older than the row is refused
   (`MESSAGE_CONTENT_CHANGED_BEFORE_SIGNING`), so a doctor never signs content changed after their page loaded. A
   caller that sends no version is not compared.
5. Unsigned content stays open to the other clinical roles exactly as before (OD-5-1 kept). Tests that exercise what
   ANOTHER clinician may author start from `RmePrescriptionFactory::unsigned()`.

## 10. A new RM sheet only on the authorized encounter (OD-5-6, owner decision 2026-11-16)

Once the patient has an RM book, `MedicalRecordService::createDraft()` refuses a new sheet on any visit other than the
encounter `assertRmeAuthoringAllowedForPatient()` returns (`MESSAGE_SHEET_ONLY_ON_ENCOUNTER`). The patient's first
sheet still starts on the canonical visit the controller derives (Sprint 64.0.2), and the canonical-book path
(`getOrCreateCanonicalMedicalRecord`, `$adoptExisting`) is not a new sheet. The sheet navigation offers "Tambah
Lembar RM" only when the source visit is the patient's current examination (`ActiveEncounterResolver::currentFor`),
still disabled until consent.

## 14. Consent integrity (OD-5-8, OD-5-9, R8A-2, rule 219 §12a) — owner decisions 2026-11-16

1. **One valid consent per visit.** `RmeVisitConsentService::sign()` refuses (`MESSAGE_DUPLICATE`) when the LOCKED visit
   already has a valid consent. Signing again is possible only after a void.
2. **Supervised void** (`rme.consents.void`, POST, `DatabaseRouteKey::PATTERN`): dedicated permission
   `void_rme_consents` (Supervisor RME; Super Admin through `Gate::before`), never the signing permission; a written
   reason of 10–1000 characters; only while the visit is `in_progress`; maker ≠ checker — the account that recorded the
   consent (`signed_by`) never voids it, decided in the service under the visit lock so it binds Super Admin too. The
   row is kept (`voided_at`, `voided_by`, `void_reason`); one `RME_CONSENT_VOIDED` audit row with ids and the reason's
   LENGTH only. After a permission change, production runs `PermissionSeeder`, then `RoleSeeder`, then
   `permission:cache-reset` (CG-15).
3. **Rule 219 §12a closed.** Inside a write transaction, `assertRmeAuthoringAllowedForPatient()` locks the encounter's
   visit row FIRST (`lockForStatusDecision`, rule 223 §6) and re-decides status and consent on it. Every consent-gated
   RME write calls it at the top of its transaction: sheet create/update/finalize (`MedicalRecordService`), handwriting
   pages (also the edit-in-place path), structured diagnoses (`MedicalRecordDiagnosisService`), the diagnosis override
   (`DiagnosisRolloutService::grantOverride`). A void or a finish committed meanwhile is seen; a later one waits.
   Odontogram charting does not depend on consent (AIR-RMC-14) and is unaffected.
4. **Authorization before validation (R8A-2).** `StoreRmeVisitConsentRequest::authorize()` and
   `VoidRmeVisitConsentRequest::authorize()` return the policy's `Gate::inspect()` response, so a refused account gets
   its 403/404 whatever its payload.
5. **Storage (OD-5-9, rule 121).** New consent signatures are written to and read from `ClinicalEvidenceStorage`
   (`RmeVisitConsentService::disk()` delegates to it; `config('rme_consent.legacy_signature_disk')` names only the
   pre-change disk for the copy command). Readers: see §14.5a (temporary read-only fallback). Objects written before
   the change are copied key-for-key, SHA-256 verified, by `rme:consent-signatures-migrate` (dry run, then `--apply`;
   never deletes — purge is a separate, owner-approved phase, rule 121 §6). The copy runs as the runtime user
   `daengtisiams` and refuses root (private disk: 0700/0600 objects owned by another user are unreadable to PHP-FPM);
   an unreadable object is never copied as empty and never overwritten; a failed write is counted, never printed. A late
   failure inside `sign()` removes the objects that call wrote (rule 224 pattern); a failure after the body finished
   keeps them.
5a. **Temporary read-only fallback (release 2; owner decision 2026-11-17; a temporary exception to rule 121 §5).**
   The owner ordered that the old read path is not disabled before the copy is migrated and verified, and that
   `--apply` waits for the owner's approval of the dry run. Until then every READER of a consent signature
   (`RmeVisitConsentService::signatureDataUri()` for the consent page/print, and the policy-gated
   `rme.consents.signature` stream) asks `signatureReadDisk()`:
   1. the clinical evidence disk whenever it holds the object — a damaged (unreadable / non-PNG) copy there answers
      "not available" (404 / no image), never the old copy;
   2. only when the clinical disk does not hold the object yet, and only while `config('rme_consent.legacy_read_fallback')`
      is true (set in config, no environment read; a missing key is false), the disk named by `legacy_signature_disk`,
      which must be on the allowlist `RmeVisitConsentService::LEGACY_SIGNATURE_READ_DISKS` (`local`) — never the public
      disk and never the clinical disk again;
   3. otherwise nothing.
   Security review (2026-11-17): only the key shape `sign()` has always written is looked up on either disk —
   `<signature_directory>/<one segment>/<one segment>.png`, relative, no `.`/`..` segment, no backslash or control
   character — so a row can never be pointed at another object of the same disk. Bytes must start with the PNG prefix
   (`\x89PNG`, as `ClinicalEvidenceStorage`) on either disk, else 404 / no image (logged without the key). The read is
   read-only: nothing is written, copied, moved or deleted; the key comes from the consent row; authorization, branch
   scope and the 404/403 contract are decided before the read, unchanged. Writers are unchanged (clinical disk only).
   Removing the fallback (`legacy_read_fallback` false, then deleting the code path) is a separate owner-approved change
   after `rme:consent-signatures-migrate --apply` has run and its SHA-256 verification is reviewed. Tests:
   `RmeVisitConsentSignatureReadFallbackTest` (FB-01…FB-18; token `RmeVisitConsent`, declared mandatory).
6. **Review round (2026-11-17).** `sign()` and `void()` re-assert the policy's branch and patient scope on the locked
   visit (CG-06 third layer, `MESSAGE_OUT_OF_SCOPE`), before any object is written; `void()` takes the same
   `lockForStatusDecision` as signing. The void form also requires a valid consent on an `in_progress` visit, because
   `Gate::before` answers `void` for Super Admin without the policy. Recorded INFO: an out-of-branch consent answers 403,
   like the existing consent `view`, not 404 (CG-05 drift kept for consistency).
7. Tests: `RmeVisitConsentSupervisedVoidTest` (token `RmeVisitConsent`, declared mandatory).

## 11. A cancelled visit is never a Kontrol prior visit (OD-5-10a)

`ClinicVisitRegistrationGuard::followUpViolation()` refuses a cancelled prior visit (read FOR SHARE under `$lock`, so a
cancellation cannot commit between the check and the insert); `ClinicVisitService::patientVisitOptions()` never
offers one; the visit page offers no "Buat Kontrol" from a cancelled visit (`canStartKontrolFromHere`, decided in the
controller). Existing links are not touched. Billing already refused a cancelled parent for the Rp0 Kontrol.

## 15. Page revision, open examination, authorization order (PR4) — owner decisions 2026-11-16

1. **OD-6-1 page revision.** Every handwriting page carries `MedicalRecord::handwritingRevision()` (a digest of its
   object key — every save writes a fresh key, rule 224 — or `none` for a page without a drawing); the key itself never
   reaches a page (rule 121 §3). The form posts `page_revision` (`none` from "+ Tambah Halaman RM");
   `MedicalRecordHandwritingService::save()` compares it with the page as stored, under the record lock and before any
   object is written, and refuses a different one with `MESSAGE_STALE_PAGE` (reload). Page 1 is read with the SAME
   selector the page renders from (`MedicalRecord::latestHandwriting()`: `saved_at` desc, `id` desc), so two page-1
   rows can never make every save stale. A request without the field is not compared. Nothing is silently replaced.
2. **OD-5-5 never two examinations in progress.** Both decisions take the patient's encounter decision lock
   (`ClinicVisitRepositoryInterface::lockPatientEncounterDecision()`, `pg_advisory_xact_lock(92_005, patient id)`)
   BEFORE they read the patient's in-progress visits, so they serialise and the second sees the first committed:
   - registration (`ClinicVisitRegistrationGuard::assertNoExaminationInProgress()`, in `ClinicVisitService::create()`):
     patient FOR SHARE (merge guard), then this lock, then each in-progress visit FOR SHARE; a new visit is refused
     while the patient has a visit `in_progress`, naming it (`MESSAGE_EXAMINATION_IN_PROGRESS`);
   - "Mulai Pemeriksaan" (`assertNoOtherExaminationInProgress()`, in `transitionStatus()`): the visit row FOR NO KEY
     UPDATE first (rule 223 §6), then this lock; refused while ANOTHER visit of the patient is in progress, naming it
     (`MESSAGE_START_WHILE_IN_PROGRESS`). Registration alone could not prevent two open examinations: two visits
     registered while neither was in progress could both be started (independent review).
   No row-lock path holds the advisory lock, so it adds no cycle; never renumber 92_005 (pinned by a test).
   Advisory lock namespaces, one lock each: 91_xxx legacy slots, 92_001 visit sequence, 92_002..92_004 the document
   numbers, 92_005 this lock; the inventory lives on `ClinicVisitRepository::PATIENT_ENCOUNTER_LOCK_NAMESPACE`, and
   the OD-5-5 pin fails if any 92_xxx literal in `app/` is declared twice or the inventory misses one.
   registered / waiting / cashier_pending visits do not block; a brand-new patient is never checked. The message may
   name a visit at another RME branch, as the OD-1 cross-branch follow-up already does.
3. **R8A-2 for prescriptions.** `StoreRmePrescriptionRequest` / `UpdateRmePrescriptionRequest::authorize()` return the
   policy's `Gate::inspect()` response, so a refused account gets 403/404 whatever its payload. The signed-lock refusal
   writes its `RME_PRESCRIPTION_SIGNED_EDIT_REJECTED` row there (§9.3), since the controller is no longer reached.
4. The OD-7-5 lab candidate backfill is §18 (its own PR).
